        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_10640105"/>
        <dc:subject><z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>固定翼编队</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag></dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Hierarchical ADP-ISMC Formation Control of Fixed-Wing UAVs in Local Frames Under Bounded Disturbances</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2024</dc:date>
        <dcterms:abstract>In this article, the problem of the fixed-wing UAV formation control in local frames is investigated, and a formation controller based on the distance constraints is designed. Without using the global position information, this article considers the GPS-denied environment, and the control of UAVs only depends on the relative range and relative angle information defined in local frames. Meanwhile, to simultaneously address the optimization and external disturbance issues of the system, an ADP-ISMC formation controller is designed in this article. Finally, the simulations are presented to illustrate the effectiveness of the proposed control method.</dcterms:abstract>
        <z:libraryCatalog>IEEE Xplore</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier><dcterms:URI><rdf:value>https://doi.org/10.1109/TSMC.2024.3429635</rdf:value></dcterms:URI></dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-11-04 11:30:00</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Article rdf:about="https://doi.org/10.1109/TAES.2026.3695512">
        <z:itemType>journalArticle</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf><bib:Journal><dc:title>IEEE Transactions on Aerospace and Electronic Systems</dc:title></bib:Journal></dcterms:isPartOf>
        <dc:publisher><foaf:Organization><foaf:name>IEEE</foaf:name></foaf:Organization></dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors><rdf:Seq><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Min</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Jie</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Yu</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Yangguang</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Cong</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Yirui</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Wang</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Xiangke</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li></rdf:Seq></bib:authors>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_11531011"/>
        <dc:subject><z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>分布式故障估计</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag></dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Distributed Fault Estimation With Noisy Measurements in Formation Control</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2026</dc:date>
        <dcterms:abstract>Accurate fault estimation in multiagent formations is critical for ensuring overall system reliability, yet it remains particularly challenging in noisy environments where conventional methods often fail. This article presents a distributed adaptive fault estimation approach and designs an adaptive observer-based estimator, leveraging local and neighboring state information for robust fault signal reconstruction under noise interference. Through rigorous Lyapunov stability analysis, we establish the global uniformly ultimately bounded convergence of estimation errors. The performance of our proposed algorithm is further verified via both numerical simulations and Gazebo experiments, demonstrating its capability to accurately estimate fault signals despite environmental noise and formation coupling effects.</dcterms:abstract>
        <z:libraryCatalog>IEEE Xplore</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier><dcterms:URI><rdf:value>https://doi.org/10.1109/TAES.2026.3695512</rdf:value></dcterms:URI></dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-11-04 11:30:00</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Article rdf:about="https://doi.org/10.1109/TSMC.2022.3220028">
        <z:itemType>journalArticle</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf><bib:Journal><dc:title>IEEE Transactions on Systems, Man, and Cybernetics: Systems</dc:title></bib:Journal></dcterms:isPartOf>
        <dc:publisher><foaf:Organization><foaf:name>IEEE</foaf:name></foaf:Organization></dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors><rdf:Seq><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Yu</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Yangguang</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Wang</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Xiangke</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Sun</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Zhiyong</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Shen</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Lincheng</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li></rdf:Seq></bib:authors>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_9953064"/>
        <dc:subject><z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>最优控制</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag></dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Optimal Control of Nonlinear Systems With Unsymmetrical Input Constraints and its Application to the UAV Circumnavigation Problem</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2023</dc:date>
        <dcterms:abstract>In this article, a novel design scheme is introduced to solve the optimal control problem for nonlinear systems with unsymmetrical and state-dependent input constraints. By introducing an initial stabilizing control policy as the baseline of the constructed optimal control policy, we remove the assumption in the current study for the adaptive optimal control, that is, the internal dynamics should hold zero when the state of the system is in the origin. Particularly, nonlinear control systems with partially unknown dynamics are investigated and the procedure to acquire the corresponding optimal control policy is presented. The stability for the closed-loop dynamics and the optimality of the obtained control policy are both proved. Besides, we apply the proposed control design framework to solve the optimal circumnavigation problem based on the accumulative Fisher information for a fixed-wing unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV). The control performance of our algorithm is compared with that of the existing circumnavigation control policy in a numerical simulation.</dcterms:abstract>
        <z:libraryCatalog>IEEE Xplore</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier><dcterms:URI><rdf:value>https://doi.org/10.1109/TSMC.2022.3220028</rdf:value></dcterms:URI></dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-11-04 11:30:00</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Article rdf:about="https://doi.org/10.1364/AO.611569">
        <z:itemType>journalArticle</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf><bib:Journal><dc:title>Applied Optics</dc:title></bib:Journal></dcterms:isPartOf>
        <dc:publisher><foaf:Organization><foaf:name>Optica</foaf:name></foaf:Organization></dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors><rdf:Seq><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Xu</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Yinbo</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Xiao</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Zhijian</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Yan</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Jiarun</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Yu</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Yangguang</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li><rdf:li><foaf:Person><foaf:surname>Wang</foaf:surname><foaf:givenName>Xiangke</foaf:givenName></foaf:Person></rdf:li></rdf:Seq></bib:authors>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_ao2026"/>
        <dc:subject><z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>视觉定位</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag></dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Robust trajectory triangulation for UAV visual localization via iteratively reweighted least squares</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2026</dc:date>
        <dcterms:abstract>Accurate localization of ground targets using monocular vision from a fixed-wing unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) in tracking mode is a challenging problem in computer vision and photogrammetry. Classical triangulation methods often suffer from rank deficiency due to degenerate observation geometry. The trajectory triangulation paradigm overcomes this limitation by parameterizing target motion, yet existing solvers typically assume Gaussian-distributed observation noise with uniform weights, making them vulnerable to non-uniform noise induced by the UAV&#x27;s high-speed maneuvering and to image matching outliers. This paper proposes a robust trajectory triangulation algorithm based on iteratively reweighted least squares. The proposed method constructs a target motion model tailored to the fixed-wing UAV&#x27;s viewing geometry and incorporates robust M-estimation kernel functions, transforming the optimization into a sequence of adaptively weighted subproblems. The algorithm dynamically adjusts the weight of each observation ray according to its residual magnitude, effectively suppressing outlier interference while preserving computational efficiency. Experiments on numerical simulation and Gazebo simulation demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms baseline linear solvers and nonlinear least-squares optimizers in localization accuracy, especially in challenging circling tracking scenarios characterized by high dynamics and noise, reducing the mean localization RMSE by 75.8% and 68.3% compared with the baseline linear solver and the nonlinear least-squares optimizer, respectively.</dcterms:abstract>
        <z:libraryCatalog>Crossref</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier><dcterms:URI><rdf:value>https://doi.org/10.1364/AO.611569</rdf:value></dcterms:URI></dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-11-04 11:30:00</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
    </bib:Article>
